## Folder layout

```
templates/TestCase_Template.xlsx          # the team's standard format (layout only)
test-cases/<JIRA-KEY>/
    <JIRA-KEY>_TestCases.xlsx             # Goal 1 output of Phase A. QA fills in the Test Steps column
    <JIRA-KEY>_Results_<yyyymmdd-hhmm>.xlsx   # Goal 1 output of Phase C. Never overwrites the input
    evidence/<yyyymmdd-hhmm>/TC<nn>_<step>.png
sql/authoring/                            # Goal 3 scripts awaiting human review
```

---

## Goal 1 — Story → test cases → execution

### Phase A: generate the test case workbook (Claude)

Trigger: QA gives a Jira key (e.g. "generate test cases for SDMS-10351") or
pastes the story export.

1. **Read the story**: summary, description (As a / I want / Given / When /
   Then), acceptance criteria, **comments** (comments often change the scope,
   e.g. "remove the previous SKU exclusion logic"), and the titles of linked
   issues (useful for the real screen name). Don't follow links beyond that.
2. **Derive test cases from the story only.** The template workbook provides
   the *format*, never the content. Cover these, where the story supports
   them:
   - positive flows for each "When" item
   - one negative case per validation rule (mandatory field blank,
     invalid/inactive code, wrong owner/entity, altered pre-filled values)
   - boundaries (start/end dates inclusive, same-day overlap vs next-day)
   - update rules (future vs current vs expired records)
   - each acceptance criterion from every entry point it names (e.g. an order
     created in BackOffice *and* one synced from Mobility)
   - the negative side of each criterion (behaviour still applies outside the
     scope/dates, or for other distributors)
   - regressions implied by comments
3. **Write the workbook** by loading `templates/TestCase_Template.xlsx`,
   clearing its sample rows, and writing into its layout. This keeps fonts,
   fills and widths identical. Rules:

   | Column | Content |
   |---|---|
   | TestCase # | Sequential number |
   | Test Case Description | Starts with "Verify…". Negative/alternate cases are prefixed **`ALT-`** |
   | Pre- Requisite | Numbered list: user/role, URL access, screen access, master data, and the specific records the case needs (e.g. "a current policy with Outlet Subtype X") |
   | Test Steps | **Blank.** QA fills this in (Phase B) |
   | Actual Result | **Blank.** Claude fills this in (Phase C) |
   | Expected Result | **Filled.** The case-level outcome, specific enough to judge pass/fail (exact message text if the story gives one) |
   | Test Status | `Not Executed`, with a dropdown: Passed/Failed/Blocked/Not Executed |
   | Test Case Evidence | Blank |
   | Version | Jira key |

   - Use one row per test case, grouped under yellow section bands (Grid,
     Download, Upload validation, Overlap, Update, Business effect, …).
   - Size row heights to fit the pre-requisite text, and freeze the header row.
   - Test Screen / Test Scenario block: use the real screen name, taken from
     the story or from linked issue titles, not from the template.
4. **Add a `Notes` sheet** listing:
   - each assumption made where the story is ambiguous or contradictory,
     quoting the story line involved
   - every case that tests behaviour the story does not state (e.g. a screen
     field known only from the existing screen). Mark these cases so a BA
     can confirm or delete them.
5. Report back: the case count per section, the assumptions, and the file
   path.

### Phase B: QA writes the test steps (human)

QA fills in **only** the Test Steps column (and may add pre-requisite test
data). Claude executes these steps literally, so write them to the
convention below.

- One action per line, numbered: `1) …`, `2) …` (Alt+Enter for a new line in
  the cell).
- Start each line with one of these verbs:

  | Verb | Example |
  |---|---|
  | Login | `1) Login` (credentials come from env vars; never put them in the sheet) |
  | Select | `2) Select Distributor = "DT-0012"` |
  | Navigate | `3) Navigate to SKU Substitution Policy` (menu path, or text for menu search) |
  | Enter | `4) Enter Description = "Auto sub excl test"` |
  | Choose | `5) Choose Type = "3 – Based on DT + Channel"` |
  | Set date | `6) Set Date From = {TODAY+1}`, `7) Set Date To = {TODAY+30}` |
  | Click | `8) Click Save` |
  | Download / Upload | `9) Download Excel for policy "P-001"`, `10) Upload file "SDMS-10351_valid.xlsx"` |
  | Edit Excel | `11) In downloaded Excel set Entity Type = "OST", Channel Hierarchy = "0101"` |
  | Verify | `12) Verify message "Policy saved Successfully"`, `Verify grid contains "P-001"` |
  | Logout | `13) Logout` |

- Put values in quotes. Use date tokens (`{TODAY}`, `{TODAY+n}`,
  `{TODAY-n}`, format `dd/MM/yyyy` unless the screen needs another format) so
  sheets don't go stale.
- Name fields by their **on-screen label**, not by id.
- Upload files live in `test-cases/<JIRA-KEY>/data/`.
- Intermediate `Verify` lines are optional. The Expected Result column is
  always checked at the end of the case.
- Leave Test Steps blank for any case that shouldn't run yet. Claude skips
  it and keeps its status `Not Executed`.

### Phase C: execute the workbook (Claude)

Trigger: QA provides the filled workbook, optionally with a filter ("run
TC 1–8", "run the Upload section").

1. **Pre-flight, before opening a browser**:
   - Check that the header row matches the template.
   - Parse every step line and resolve the date tokens.
   - List the cases that will run, the cases skipped (no steps), and any step
     line that doesn't fit the convention or is ambiguous (missing value,
     unknown verb, unclear target).
   - Show this summary to QA. Ask about ambiguous lines; don't guess them.
2. **Confirm the environment**: the base URL must be a test/QA environment,
   never production. Steps create real records (policies, uploads, orders).
3. **Run each test case in a fresh session**: open the browser, log in, run
   the steps in order, check the Expected Result, log out, close the
   browser. Never reuse a session or state from a previous case.
4. **Execute each step literally** using Selenium MCP. Find elements through
   the accessibility snapshot using the step's on-screen label; see the
   DevExtreme locator caution under Goal 3. Take a screenshot after every
   `Verify` step, and on failure.
5. **Set the status** for each case:
   - **Passed**: all steps succeeded and the Expected Result was observed.
   - **Failed**: the app behaved differently from a Verify line or the
     Expected Result. Record exactly what was observed.
   - **Blocked**: the case couldn't be evaluated. Causes include a missing
     pre-requisite record, a step that couldn't be performed as written
     (element not found, ambiguous), or the environment being down. Record
     the step number and reason, plus a suggested rewrite of the step when
     relevant.
   - Never change, add to, or "fix" QA's steps to make a case pass. Report
     the problem as Blocked.
6. **Write results to a copy** (`<JIRA-KEY>_Results_<timestamp>.xlsx`).
   Keep the original workbook untouched. In the copy:
   - Actual Result: a short summary of what was observed, and the failing
     step number if any.
   - Test Status: the status from step 5.
   - Test Case Evidence: hyperlinks to the screenshots.
7. **Report back**: counts of Passed/Failed/Blocked/Not Executed, a
   one-line reason for each Failed/Blocked case, and the results file path.
8. **Optional bridge to Goal 3**: when a case passes, its proven steps and
   locators can seed a Regress Master flow. Do this only if QA asks, and
   still as reviewed SQL.

---

## Goal 2 — Execute an existing Regress Master test flow

Trigger: QA names a flow (id or description, e.g. "run Dispatch Advice").

1. Query `selenium-framework-db` for the flow: `fct_pr_tf_test_flow` →
   `fct_pr_tfd_test_flow_details` (ordered menu groups) → `fct_pr_mg_menu_group`
   (`pmg_searchtext` + `pmg_navigation`/`pmg_navigation_type` = how to reach
   it) → `fct_pr_sc_screens` per group → `fct_pr_sf_screen_field` (fields to
   fill) + `fct_pr_sef_screen_events_flow` (ordered steps to run).
2. **Only include event-flow rows where `psef_status = 'Y'`** — confirmed
   against the legacy engine's own code
   (`DBExecution.initialiseScreenEventsCache()`): it filters
   `WHERE PSEF_Status in ('Y','A')`, and `A` is unused in practice (0 rows
   live) — so `N` rows are genuinely inactive, not just annotated. Don't
   execute steps whose status isn't `Y`.
3. Launch Selenium MCP, log in, replay the config live: menu-rollin → type
   search text → click the target, per group; then per screen, fill fields
   and run active events in `psef_sequenceno` order.
4. Report pass/fail per screen/step. Log out, close the session.

## Goal 3 — Author a new Regress Master test flow

Trigger: QA describes a scenario in plain English (menu path, what to fill,
what "success" looks like) because no flow id exists yet, or asks to convert
a passing Goal 1 test case.

1. Drive Selenium MCP live through exactly what was described, discovering
   real locators as you go (accessibility snapshot, not guesswork).
2. **Locator caution specific to this app**: S&D uses DevExtreme grids whose
   ids are regenerated per render (`dx-<hex-guid>` pattern, or similar
   auto-generated ids like `dx-col-285` seen in existing config). NEVER
   author a script around one of these — build the locator from a stable
   attribute instead (`aria-label`, column header text, role). A cached GUID
   id is guaranteed to break on the next page load. The same rule applies to
   Goal 1 execution.
3. Record what actually happened as you go — element locators discovered,
   and the exact sequence executed — so the eventual SQL is traceable back to
   something that was actually proven live, not inferred.
4. Generate the SQL script (`fct_pr_mg_menu_group` / `fct_pr_sc_screens` /
   `fct_pr_sf_screen_field` / `fct_pr_sef_screen_events_flow` /
   `fct_pr_tf_test_flow` / `fct_pr_tfd_test_flow_details`, matching this
   schema's actual columns — verify with `get_table_schema` first, it
   differs slightly from any newer/parallel framework you may also have
   docs for). Every field/event row must trace to a locator actually
   exercised in step 1-3, not an invented one.
5. **Hand the script to a human for review. Do not apply it.** This is
   non-negotiable — same rule as reading the DB read-only.
6. Log out, close the session.

## Conventions

- **Read-only against `CTA_CONFIG`** — the DB connector should only ever run
  SELECTs; if it accepts writes, that's a misconfiguration to fix before
  using it for this work.
- **Never auto-apply generated SQL** — always a reviewed file, always a human
  runs it.
- **Never overwrite QA's workbook** — results always go to a timestamped copy.
- **Template is format, not content** — never copy test cases from
  `TestCase_Template.xlsx` or older workbooks into a new story's workbook.
- **Don't invent test steps or test data at execution time** — ask during
  pre-flight, or mark the case Blocked.
- **Test environments only** — Goal 1 execution creates real records; never
  point it at production.
- **Jira is read-only** unless QA explicitly asks for a comment/transition.
- **Never commit or echo real credentials** — S&D login creds, DB creds, etc.
  go in a gitignored local secrets file / env vars, never in a script,
  workbook, or chat output.
- **Verify schema before assuming column names** — this legacy schema and
  any newer rewrite of it can drift; call `get_table_schema` rather than
  assuming a column exists.
- Before trusting any DB query result, confirm which literal database you're
  connected to (`SELECT current_database()`) — there are several
  similarly-named sibling databases on the same server
  (`CTA_CONFIG_DEV`, `CTA_QA`, `CTA_CONFIG_ASSERTION`, personal dev clones,
  etc.) and querying the wrong one silently returns plausible-looking but
  wrong data.

## Headed vs headless

**Headed for all goals**, by team preference. QA wants to watch the
automation run live, not just get a pass/fail report. This also has a real
technical benefit: watching the browser is how you catch a bad locator guess
before it becomes a wrong result or a wrong SQL script, especially around the
DevExtreme grids called out above.

## Environment variables (fill in before use)

- `SD_BASE_URL` — S&D start URL (confirm the real one; do not trust the
  placeholder above). Must be a test/QA environment.
- `SD_TEST_USER` / `SD_TEST_PASSWORD` — login credentials (secrets file, not
  committed). Used by the `Login` step.
- `SD_DEFAULT_DISTRIBUTOR` — used when a `Select Distributor` step has no
  value (optional; otherwise the case is Blocked).
- Any other per-flow warehouse/vendor defaults your team wants as fallbacks,
  mirroring how a sibling project keys `GIAS_DEFAULT_BRANCH` etc.

## Open items before this is fully usable

- [ ] Confirm `CTA_CONFIG` is the correct live config DB (not a dev/QA/clone
      variant) with whoever owns it
- [ ] Confirm the real S&D base URL and environment
- [ ] Decide where login credentials come from
- [ ] Decide where generated SQL scripts get written/reviewed (a
      `sql/authoring/`-style folder, PR review, etc.)
- [ ] Decide element-locator cache location/format if you want the
      self-healing/reuse benefit this pattern gives elsewhere
- [ ] Commit the team's standard test case workbook as
      `templates/TestCase_Template.xlsx`
- [ ] Agree the Test Steps verb list and date-token format with QA (Phase B)
- [ ] Decide whether Jira is read through the Atlassian connector or via
      pasted exports
- [ ] Decide who owns test data setup for pre-requisites (QA manually, or
      extra setup steps inside each case)
- [ ] Decide where screenshots/evidence are kept long-term (local folder,
      Jira attachment, shared drive)
